A mathematical model based on the responses obtained from experiments, was developed using Minitab software that represents the GRG as a function of independent variables including cutting speed, feed, depth of cut, tool coatings, and amplitude. Machining parameters including cutting speed, feed, depth of cut and amplitude were considered as continuous factors while tool coatings as non-continuous categorical factors for optimization in the current study. The regression model for GRG based on RSM is shown in Eq. ( 5 ) through ( 8 ). Although the GRG regression equations corresponding to different tool coatings exhibit similar mathematical structure, this is a direct consequence of employing the same experimental framework and consistent underlying cutting mechanisms across all tool conditions. The influence of tool coating is primarily reflected through shifts in the regression constants, while the governing functional form and interaction structure remain consistent across tools. Consequently, each tool condition yields a distinct GRG response surface and optimal parameter combination, as validated by GRG optimization and experimental results. 5 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
